## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Your code uses it to kill a thread that exceeds a deadline. The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with cooperative cancellation.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T, CancellationToken> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Overload for work that cannot accept a token (fire-and-forget on timeout)
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | The `Func<T>` overload abandons the task but it keeps running in the background. Only the token-aware overload actually stops work. |
| **Must pass token through the call chain** | `work` must call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints or pass `token` to async I/O. |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using` — leaking it leaks internal timers. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). |
| **Truly uncooperative code** | If `work` calls blocking code you can't modify (native call, third-party lib), move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()` — there is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |

---

### Caller Migration Example

**Before (.NET Framework):**
```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => ComputeExpensiveResult(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

**After (.NET 8) — cooperative:**
```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        // ... do chunk of work ...
    }
    return finalResult;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

The fundamental shift: cancellation is now **cooperative** — the work must voluntarily check the token. There is no way to forcibly tear down a managed thread in modern .NET.
